"Common billing questions and troubleshooting methods - Expenses and Costs - Alibaba Cloud Documentation Center (https://www.alibabacloud.com/help/en/user-center/bill-solution-troubleshooting-manual-for-common-consumer-bill-questions)\nciteturn45search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: today; Object Storage Service (OSS) — Deleting individual files from a bucket does not stop billing. ... Resource-holding services (such as OSS, snapshots, Cloud Storage Gateway (CSG), and Vector Retrieval Service (VRS)) — As long as the resource exists — for example, a bucket contains data, a snapshot has not been deleted, or a gateway instance has been created — charges continue to accrue based on storage volume or instance duration. ... Deletion is irreversible — once deleted, an API key cannot be recovered or used again, and the final billing cycle statement still applies after deletion. ... Billing after the free quota is exhausted — For products such as LLMs, after the free quota expires or is fully consumed, continued usage automatically switches to pay-as-you-go billing and charges are deducted from your account balance.\nBoth continue to incur charges. To stop charges, in the ECS console, go to Storage & Snapshots > Snapshots, delete all snapshots, and remove any associated automatic snapshot policies.\n\n  2. Object Storage Service (OSS) — Deleting individual files from a bucket does not stop billing. You must clear all objects, incomplete multipart upload fragments, and all historical object versions from the bucket, then delete the bucket itself. Manage your buckets in the OSS console.\n\n  3. Elastic IP Address (EIP) — An EIP that is not bound to any resource incurs an IP retention fee. You must actively release the EIP to stop charges. In the VPC console, go to Elastic IP Addresses to release any unassociated EIPs.\n\n  4. Function Compute, SAE, ApsaraDB RDS, and ApsaraDB for Redis — Stopping an application or instance is not the same as releasing it. Stopped resources continue to incur charges. To stop billing, perform a Release Instance or Delete Application operation in the respective product console.\n\n  5. Elastic Desktop Service (EDS) premium public bandwidth — Shutting down an EDS instance does not release the associated premium public network bandwidth resource, which continues to incur charges. To stop billing, unbind the office network connection first, then delete the bandwidth resource in the EDS console.\n\n\n## Pay-as-you-go charges without active usage\n\nWhether a pay-as-you-go product generates charges without active usage depends on its metering method:\n\n  1. API call-based services (such as TINGWU, Zhizuo Workshop, and Model Studio web search plugin) — These services use a pure pay-as-you-go model. Charges are incurred only when an API call is successfully made or a feature is actively used. If you have not made any API calls or enabled any related plugins, no charges are generated — even if the service has been activated.\n\n  2. Resource-holding services (such as OSS, snapshots, Cloud Storage Gateway (CSG), and Vector Retrieval Service (VRS)) — As long as the resource exists — for example, a bucket contains data, a snapshot has not been deleted, or a gateway instance has been created — charges continue to accrue based on storage volume or instance duration. Activating the service without creating an instance or uploading any data does not generate charges.\n\n  3. Model Studio API keys — Activating Model Studio without making any model calls does not generate charges. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBilling | IBM Cloud Docs (https://cloud.ibm.com/docs/cloud-object-storage?locale=en&topic=cloud-object-storage-billing)\nciteturn45search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; For each storage class, billing is based on aggregated usage across all buckets at the instance level. ... Storage costs for IBM Cloud® Object Storage are determined by the average monthly stored volume of data, the amount of public outbound bandwidth used, and the total number of operational requests processed by the system. ... Use of the Object Storage Backup feature does not incur any operational, retrieval, or egress charges on the source bucket. ... More information can be found at the documentation\n# Billing\n\nBilling\n\n# Billing\n\nInformation on pricing can be found at IBM Cloud®.\n\n## Invoices\n\nFind your account invoices at Manage > Billing and Usage in the navigation menu.\n\nUnder a Standard plan, service instance receives a single bill. If you need separate billing for different sets of buckets, then creating multiple instances is necessary.\n\nFor each storage class, billing is based on aggregated usage across all buckets at the instance level. For example, for Smart Tier, the billing is based on usage across all Smart Tier buckets in a given instance - not on the individual buckets.\n\n## IBM Cloud Object Storage pricing\n\nStorage costs for IBM Cloud® Object Storage are determined by the average monthly stored volume of data, the amount of public outbound bandwidth used, and the total number of operational requests processed by the system.\n\n## Request classes\n\n'Class A' requests involve modification or listing. This category includes creating buckets, uploading or copying objects, creating or changing configurations, listing buckets, and listing the contents of buckets.\n\n'Class B' requests are related to retrieving objects or their associated metadata or configurations from the system.\n\nDeleting buckets or objects from the system does not incur a charge. For charges related to Multiple Deletes, see Delete multiple objects.\n\nRequest classes\nClass  | Requests  | Examples\n--- | --- | ---\nClass A  | PUT, COPY, and POST requests, as well as GET requests used to list buckets and objects  | Creating buckets, uploading or copying objects, listing buckets, listing contents of buckets, setting ACLs, and setting CORS configurations\nClass B  | GET (excluding listing), HEAD, and OPTIONS requests  | Retrieving objects and metadata\n\nRequests made using the Resource Configuration API are not charged for requests and do not accrue usage for billing purposes.\n\n## Aspera transfers\n\nAspera high-speed transfer incurs extra egress charges. For more information, see the pricing page.\n\n## Backup billing\n\nThere are two charges for use of the Object Storage Backup service. The first charge is for the amount of data stored within the Backup Vault. This is measured in Gigabytes. Usage is averaged over the billing period. There is an additional second charge for any restores initiated from the Backup Vault during the billing period. Restore jobs incur a fee for each gigabyte of data that is restored.\n\nUse of the Object Storage Backup feature does not incur any operational, retrieval, or egress charges on the source bucket. There are also no operational charges for writing backups to the Backup Vault.\n\nThe target bucket of a restore job will incur operational charges for the data written to the bucket on restore.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBilling rules for Cloud Storage Gateway - Cloud Storage Gateway - Alibaba Cloud Documentation Center (https://www.alibabacloud.com/help/en/csg/product-overview/billing-1)\nciteturn45search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago;     * If the gateway specifications, cache capacity, and Internet bandwidth are the same, the fee for a cross-zone high availability file gateway is twice that of a Basic Edition file gateway. ... If you enable the server-side encryption feature for OSS, OSS calls the KMS API to perform operations such as querying, generating, encrypting, and decrypting data keys. ... Cloud Storage Gateway  | When creating a Cloud Storage Gateway, determine whether to use the subscription billing method for the gateway resources. ...   * For pay-as-you-go Cloud Storage Gateways, billing stops after you delete the gateway.\n\n# Cloud Storage Gateway:Billing rules for Cloud Storage Gateway\n\nLast Updated:Aug 24, 2026\n\nThis topic describes the billing for Cloud Storage Gateway, including billable items, billing methods, and overdue payments.\n\n## Billable items\n\nCloud Storage Gateway includes online gateways and on-premises gateways. The billable items for each type are different.\n\n### Online gateways\n\nBilling for online gateways is based on gateway specifications, cache type, and Internet bandwidth. Both pay-as-you-go and subscription billing methods are supported. The fees for the two billing methods are calculated as follows:\n\n  * Pay-as-you-go: Hourly gateway fee = Hourly price of the gateway specification + Cache capacity (GB) × Price per GB/hour + Internet bandwidth (Mbps) × Price per Mbps/hour.\n\n  * Subscription: Monthly/Yearly price = Monthly/Yearly price of the gateway specification + Cache capacity (GB) × Monthly/Yearly price per GB + Internet bandwidth (Mbps) × Monthly/Yearly price per Mbps.\n\nNote\n\nBy default, Internet bandwidth is not enabled for a gateway. \n  2. On the Gateway List page, find the desired Cloud Storage Gateway and choose More > Switch to Subscription.\n\n  3. On the Cloud Storage Gateway (Subscription) page, select a billing cycle and click Buy Now.\n\nNote\n\nOn the Cloud Storage Gateway (Subscription) page, select a billing cycle. Do not modify the other configuration items.\n\n  4. Complete the payment as prompted on the page.\n\n## Overdue payments\n\nAfter a Cloud Storage Gateway has an overdue payment, its service status changes as follows:\n\n  * For a Cloud Storage Gateway deployed in a data center\n\nAfter 24 hours of an overdue payment, Alibaba Cloud pauses the service and freezes operations for the Cloud Storage Gateway. Data stored on the local cache disk of the Cloud Storage Gateway is retained but cannot be read. If you pay the overdue amount within 15 days after the service is stopped, the Cloud Storage Gateway service is automatically restored. You can then continue to access the data retained on the local cache disk through the Cloud Storage Gateway.\n\n  * For a Cloud Storage Gateway deployed on Alibaba Cloud\n\n    * After 24 hours of an overdue payment, Alibaba Cloud pauses the service and freezes operations for the Cloud Storage Gateway. However, the data you retain on the Cloud Storage Gateway cache disk continues to be stored and billed.\n\n    * After 15 days of service suspension (from the time the service is paused to the same time on the 15th day), Alibaba Cloud stops providing the service. At the same time, all data that you have saved on the Cloud Storage Gateway cache disk is deleted and cleared, and cannot be recovered.\n\n## Refund policy\n\n  * For pay-as-you-go Cloud Storage Gateways, billing stops after you delete the gateway.\n\nNote\n\nFees that have already been generated are not refundable after billing stops.\n\n  * For subscription Cloud Storage Gateways that have not expired, you can request a refund. For more information, see Unsubscribe from a product.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAccess tiers for blob data - Azure Storage | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/access-tiers-overview)\nciteturn45search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; All storage accounts use a pricing model for block blob storage that is based on a blob's tier. ... An additional data write (per GB) charge applies when moving blobs to a cooler tier within a legacy Blob Storage account. ... Early deletion charges for any blob moved out of the cool, cold or archive tier may apply as well. ... For information about blobs with versioning enabled, see Pricing and billing in the blob versioning documentation.\n\nOutbound data transfers (data that is transferred out of an Azure region) incur billing for bandwidth usage on a per-gigabyte basis. For more information on outbound data transfer charges, see Bandwidth Pricing Details page.\n\n### Changing the default account access tier\n\nChanging the account access tier results in tier change charges for all blobs that don't already have a tier explicitly set. For more information, see the following section, Changing a blob's access tier.\n\n### Changing a blob's access tier\n\nKeep in mind the following billing impacts when changing a blob's tier:\n\n  * When a blob is uploaded or moved between tiers, it's charged at the corresponding rate immediately upon upload or tier change.\n  * When a blob is moved to a cooler tier, the operation is billed as a write operation (per 10,000) to the destination tier. An additional data write (per GB) charge applies when moving blobs to a cooler tier within a legacy Blob Storage account.\n  * When a blob is moved to a warmer tier, the operation is billed as a read from the source tier, where the read operation (per 10,000) and data retrieval (per GB) charges of the source tier apply. Early deletion charges for any blob moved out of the cool, cold or archive tier may apply as well.\n  * While a blob is being rehydrated from the archive tier, that blob's data is billed as archived data until the data is restored and the blob's tier changes to hot, cool, or cold.\n\nThe following table summarizes how tier changes are billed.\n\nExpand table\n\nWrite charges (operation + access)  | Read charges (operation + access)\n--- | ---\nHot to cool\nHot to cold\nHot to archive\nCool to cold\nCool to archive\nCold to archive  | Archive to cold\nArchive to cool\nArchive to hot\nCold to cool\nCold to hot\nCool to hot\n\nChanging the access tier for a blob when versioning is enabled, or if the blob has snapshots, might result in more charges. For information about blobs with versioning enabled, see Pricing and billing in the blob versioning documentation. For information about blobs with snapshots, see Pricing and billing in the blob snapshots documentation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUser Guide (https://support.huaweicloud.com/intl/en-us/usermanual-obs/Object%20Storage%20Service%20User%20Guide-pdf.pdf)\nciteturn45search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Object Storage ServiceUser Guide                                                                 8 Data Management ... <td>If objects are deleted before they have been stored for the required minimum duration (180, 90, and 30 days for Deep Archive, Archive, and Infrequent Access storage, respectively), you still need to pay for the remaining days.</td> ... <td>After the deletion is executed, objects have been deleted, so no more billing applies.</td>\nObject Storage Service  \nUser Guide                                                                 8 Data Management\n\n**Table 8-2 Billing for lifecycle management actions (during action execution)**\n\n<table id=\"e1\">\n<tr>\n<th>Action</th>\n<th>Billing Item</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n<th>Billing Mode</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delete after expiration</td>\n<td>Requests</td>\n<td>You are billed for the number of successfully deleted objects.</td>\n<td>Pay-per-Use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delete after expiration</td>\n<td>Storage space</td>\n<td>If objects are deleted before they have been stored for the required minimum duration (180, 90, and 30 days for Deep Archive, Archive, and Infrequent Access storage, respectively), you still need to pay for the remaining days.</td>\n<td>Pay-per-Use<br>Resource packages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transition from Standard to Infrequent Access</td>\n<td>Infrequent Access PUT requests</td>\n<td>You are billed for the number of successfully transitioned objects. If N objects are successfully transitioned, you are billed for N requests.</td>\n<td>Pay-per-Use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transition from Standard to Archive</td>\n<td>Archive PUT requests</td>\n<td>You are billed for the number of successfully transitioned objects. If N objects are successfully transitioned, you are billed for N requests.</td>\n<td>Pay-per-Use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transition from Infrequent Access to Archive</td>\n<td>Archive PUT requests</td>\n<td>You are billed for the number of successfully transitioned objects. If N objects are successfully transitioned, you are billed for N requests.</td>\n<td>Pay-per-Use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transition from Standard to Deep Archive</td>\n<td>Deep Archive PUT requests</td>\n<td>You are billed for the number of successfully transitioned objects. If N objects are successfully transitioned, you are billed for N requests.</td>\n<td>Pay-per-Use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transition from Infrequent Access to Deep Archive</td>\n<td>Deep Archive PUT requests</td>\n<td>You are billed for the number of successfully transitioned objects. If N objects are successfully transitioned, you are billed for N requests.</td>\n<td>Pay-per-Use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transition from Archive to Deep Archive</td>\n<td>Deep Archive PUT requests</td>\n<td>You are billed for the number of successfully transitioned objects. If N objects are successfully transitioned, you are billed for N requests.</td>\n<td>Pay-per-Use</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n\n**Table 8-3 Billing for lifecycle management actions (after action execution)**\n\n<table id=\"e2\">\n<tr>\n<th>Action</th>\n<th>Billing Item</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n<th>Billing Mode</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delete after expiration</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>After the deletion is executed, objects have been deleted, so no more billing applies.</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n\nIssue 01 (2026-01-19)                                                                 Copyright © Huawei Cloud Computing Technologies Co., Ltd.                                                                 474--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOSS billing: billable items, pricing, and payment methods - Object Storage Service - Alibaba Cloud Documentation Center (https://www.alibabacloud.com/help/en/oss/billing-overview)\nciteturn45search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 3 days ago; Value-added services such as CDN acceleration, image processing, Transfer Acceleration, and DDoS protection generate additional billable items. ... Use the billable item or billable item code of the charge to identify the storage class and data redundancy type actually used by the bucket. ... Bills are generated on an hourly basis, and there is a delay between when usage occurs and when it appears on your bill. ... Although the Object Storage Service pricing page lists the unit price for storage in `USD/GB/month`, pay-as-you-go billing is calculated by using the formula: `Actual resource usage × Hourly unit price`.\n\n# Object Storage Service:Billing overview\n\nLast Updated:Sep 04, 2026\n\nOSS charges on a pay-as-you-go basis.\n\nStoring data in OSS incurs a storage fee based on duration. Each upload, download, or access incurs a request fee, and public internet distribution incurs outbound data transfer fees. Value-added services such as CDN acceleration, image processing, Transfer Acceleration, and DDoS protection generate additional billable items.\n\nIf your workload has predictable usage, purchase a resource plan to reduce costs. A resource plan must precisely match the billable item it covers. For example, a data transfer plan offsets only outbound data transfer fees. A mismatched plan will not apply to your bill.\n\n## Pricing\n\nFull pricing details are on the OSS Product Pricing.\n\n## Billable items\n\nOSS has two categories of billable items:\n\n  * Basic billable items: Common charges for most OSS users, including the storage fee for stored data, outbound data transfer fees for downloads or CDN distribution, and the request fee for read and write requests.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPay-as-you-go - Object Storage Service - Alibaba Cloud Documentation Center (https://www.alibabacloud.com/help/en/oss/pay-as-you-go-1)\nciteturn45search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: today; Pay-as-you-go is the default billing method for Object Storage Service (OSS).With pay-as-you-go, OSS bills you for the resources that you actually use, and the fees for each supported billable item are calculated and settled on a fixed cycle. ... If your available credit is less than the amount to be settled, you receive a text message or an email notification about the insufficient balance.\n\n# Object Storage Service:Pay-as-you-go\n\nLast Updated:Aug 27, 2026\n\nPay-as-you-go is the default billing method for Object Storage Service (OSS). With pay-as-you-go, OSS bills you for the resources that you actually use, and the fees for each supported billable item are calculated and settled on a fixed cycle.\n\n## Billing rule\n\nUnder the pay-as-you-go billing method, OSS charges you based on your actual resource usage. You do not need to purchase OSS resources before you use them, and resources that you do not use are not billed.\n\n## Scenarios\n\nThe pay-as-you-go billing method is suitable for the following scenarios:\n\n  * Fluctuating usage — Your OSS resource usage changes frequently.\n\n  * Short-term usage — You need OSS resources for a limited period only, such as for a temporary or unplanned workload.\n\n## Billable items\n\nThe following OSS billable items support the pay-as-you-go billing method:\n\n  * Storage fees\n\n  * Traffic fees\n\n  * Request fees\n\n  * Data processing fees\n\n  * Object tagging fees\n\n  * Transfer acceleration fees\n\n  * Temporary storage fees\n\n## Billing cycle\n\nOSS calculates pay-as-you-go fees on an hourly basis and settles them on a monthly basis. At the end of each monthly billing cycle, OSS generates a bill and deducts the fees from your Alibaba Cloud account.\n\nNote\n\nThe available credit of your Alibaba Cloud account includes your account balance and your vouchers. If your available credit is less than the amount to be settled, you receive a text message or an email notification about the insufficient balance.\n\n## Pricing\n\nFor information about pay-as-you-go pricing for OSS, see OSS pricing.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n📦 Introducing ic-blob-storage: a storage service foundation for Internet Computer apps - Developers - Internet Computer Developer Forum (https://forum.dfinity.org/t/introducing-ic-blob-storage-a-storage-service-foundation-for-internet-computer-apps/75786)\nciteturn45search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Caffeine is the current integration candidate. ...   * Billing cessation: What proves that an object’s remaining charges have ended? ... The initial scope is a bounded storage journey with clear ownership and recovery rules. ... This protects against delayed deletion confirmations being applied to a newer object with the same root.\n\n## Image: :hot_beverage: What about the storage provider?\n\nCaffeine is the current integration candidate. The repository includes a reviewed integration baseline and local decoding and validation for selected provider replies.\n\nProvider qualification remains open. In particular, the project still needs a supported independent onboarding arrangement and reliable evidence for completion, recovery, and settlement.\n\nThe provider questions we still need to close\n\nThe important questions are concrete:\n\n  * Ownership: Which account, project, or namespace belongs to the service, and who controls its callbacks?\n  * Upload completion: After a lost response, how can we determine whether the original upload completed?\n  * Payments: After an uncertain top-up, how can we establish the accepted or refunded amount for that exact operation?\n  * Deletion: What evidence ties a deletion confirmation to the correct object and operation?\n  * Recovery: Which authoritative records survive the supported backup and restore boundary?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow to Destroy a DigitalOcean Spaces Bucket | DigitalOcean Documentation (https://docs.digitalocean.com/products/spaces/how-to/destroy/)\nciteturn45search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Spaces Object Storage is an S3-compatible service for storing and serving large amounts of data. ... If you delete your last remaining bucket, your Spaces subscription automatically ends and billing stops. ... It remains listed under the Buckets tab on the Spaces Object Storage page with an estimated deletion time.\n\n# How to Destroy a DigitalOcean Spaces Bucket\n\nLast verified 13 Jul 2026\n\nSpaces Object Storage is an S3-compatible service for storing and serving large amounts of data. The built-in Spaces CDN minimizes page load times, improves performance, and reduces bandwidth and infrastructure costs.\n\nCopy page as Markdown View page as Markdown\n\nDeleting a Spaces bucket permanently and irreversibly removes the bucket and all of its contents. If you delete your last remaining bucket, your Spaces subscription automatically ends and billing stops.\n\nBucket deletion is processed asynchronously and may take several days, depending on the amount of data. During this time, the bucket name cannot be reused. You are not billed for the bucket while deletion is in progress.\n\n## Destroy Your Bucket Using the Control Panel\n\nTo destroy a bucket, go to the control panel, on the left menu, click Spaces Object Storage, and then in the Buckets tab, click the bucket you want to destroy.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBillable items of OSS storage usage-Object Storage Service(OSS)-阿里云帮助中心 (https://help.aliyun.com/en/oss/storage-fees)\nciteturn45search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last week; Storage usage of Cold Archive LRS objects that are stored for less than the minimum storage duration  | EarlyDeletionCA  | The minimum storage duration is calculated based on the time when the storage class of the object is converted to Cold Archive or Deep Cold Archive. ... ## Billing examples ... If you notice that your OSS storage capacity has increased unexpectedly, you can troubleshoot the issue using the following steps: ... ### After I purchase an IA (LRS) storage plan, why do I receive a large bill for the storage usage of IA LRS objects that are stored for less than the minimum storage duration in a specific billing cycle? ... In this case, the metering data of this billable item (LessthanMonthDatasize) is 1 TB × (720 - 24) = 696 TB. ...   * If you delete an object in this scenario, you are charged only once for the remaining duration.\n\n# Storage fees\n\nUpdated at: 2026-09-09 06:01:38\n\nCopy as MD\n\nProduct\n\nMy Favorites\n\nOSS charges storage fees based on the storage class, size, and duration of objects in a bucket, regardless of access or operations performed.\n\n## Unit price\n\nFor unit prices of each billable item, see .OSS product pricing\n\nThe OSS product pricing page specifies storage unit prices in `CNY per GB-month`, but billing items are calculated hourly using the formula: `Actual resource usage × Hourly unit price`. Therefore, to calculate actual storage fees, you need to convert the storage unit price to `CNY per GB-hour`. For example, the Standard (LRS) unit price is `0.12 CNY/GB/month`, so the hourly unit price is approximately `0.000167 CNY/GB/hour (0.12 ÷ 30 ÷ 24)`.\n\n## Billable items\n\n  * Storage fees for a specified storage duration\n\nStorage fees are based on the storage class, redundancy type, size, and duration of each object.\n\n  * Storage fees for early deletion (less than minimum duration)\n\nSome storage classes have a minimum storage duration: 30 days for Infrequent Access (IA), 60 days for Archive, and 180 days for Cold Archive and Deep Cold Archive. If an object is converted or deleted before meeting the minimum duration, you are charged for the remaining duration.\n\nExample: If an IA LRS object is deleted after less than 30 days (720 hours), you are charged for the remainder (720 hours - actual duration). The same calculation applies to Archive, Cold Archive, and Deep Cold Archive objects with their respective minimum durations.\n\n### Storage usage of LRS objects that are stored for a specific period of time\n\nBillable item  | Code  | Minimum billable size\nBillable item  | Code  | Minimum billable size\nStorage usage of Standard LRS objects  | Storage  | N/A(billed by actual size)\nStorage usage of IA LRS objects  | ChargedDatasize  | 64 KB(Objects smaller than 64 KB are billed as 64 KB. Objects 64 KB or larger are billed by actual size.)\nStorage usage of Archive LRS objects  | ChargedDatasize\nStorage usage of Cold Archive LRS objects  | ChargedDatasizeCA\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBlobs | Walrus Docs (https://docs.wal.app/oyster/json-api/blobs)\nciteturn45search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago;   * Pool-scoped expiration: blobs share their account's `StoragePool` lifetime; a background extension service renews each pool before it expires (see Blob Lifecycle) ... Tags are replaced on every PUT, so re-uploading a key without any `x-oyster-tag` headers clears its tags. ... The underlying data is only removed from storage when no other keys reference the same content (reference-counted deletion). ... `key`  | string  | Object key to delete\n\n# Blobs\n\nBlobs are binary objects stored inside buckets. Each blob is identified by a user-chosen key (like a file path) and has a content-addressed blob ID computed from its contents.\n\nKey properties:\n\n  * Reads are public: no authentication needed to download a blob\n  * Writes require auth: uploading, updating, and deleting need a Bearer token\n  * Overwrite semantics: uploading to an existing key replaces the blob\n  * Content-addressed: identical content is stored only once\n  * Reference-counted deletion: underlying data is removed only when no keys reference it\n  * Pool-scoped expiration: blobs share their account's `StoragePool` lifetime; a background extension service renews each pool before it expires (see Blob Lifecycle)\n\n## Store Blob​\n    \n    `PUT /api/v1/buckets/{bucket_name}/blobs/{key}\n    `\n\nUploads binary data to the specified bucket and key. If a blob already exists at that key, it is replaced.\n\nPath parameters:\n\nParameter  | Type  | Description\n--- | --- | ---\n`bucket_name`  | string  | Target bucket\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nObject Gateway Guide | Red Hat Ceph Storage | 6 | Red Hat Documentation (https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_ceph_storage/6/html-single/object_gateway_guide/object_gateway_guide)\nciteturn45search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Garbage collection is a background activity that runs continuously or during times of low loads, depending upon how the storage administrator configures the Ceph Object Gateway. ... Since garbage collection operations are a normal function of the Ceph Object Gateway, especially with object delete operations, objects eligible for garbage collection exist most of the time.\n### 8.12. Optimize the Ceph Object Gateway’s garbage collection\n\nCopy link Link copied to clipboard!\n\nWhen new data objects are written into the storage cluster, the Ceph Object Gateway immediately allocates the storage for these new objects. After you delete or overwrite data objects in the storage cluster, the Ceph Object Gateway deletes those objects from the bucket index. Some time afterward, the Ceph Object Gateway then purges the space that was used to store the objects in the storage cluster. The process of purging the deleted object data from the storage cluster is known as Garbage Collection, or GC.\n\nGarbage collection operations typically run in the background. You can configure these operations to either run continuously, or to run only during intervals of low activity and light workloads. By default, the Ceph Object Gateway conducts GC operations continuously. Because GC operations are a normal part of Ceph Object Gateway operations, deleted objects that are eligible for garbage collection exist most of the time.\nThe process of purging the deleted object data from the Red Hat Ceph Storage cluster is known as garbage collection (GC).\n\nViewing the objects awaiting garbage collection can be done with the following command:\n    \n    `radosgw-admin gc list`\n\nGarbage collection is a background activity that runs continuously or during times of low loads, depending upon how the storage administrator configures the Ceph Object Gateway. By default, the Ceph Object Gateway conducts garbage collection operations continuously. Since garbage collection operations are a normal function of the Ceph Object Gateway, especially with object delete operations, objects eligible for garbage collection exist most of the time.\n\nSome workloads can temporarily or permanently outpace the rate of garbage collection activity. This is especially true of delete-heavy workloads, where many objects get stored for a short period of time and then deleted. For these types of workloads, storage administrators can increase the priority of garbage collection operations relative to other operations with the following configuration parameters:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nObject Storage Service (https://support.huaweicloud.com/intl/en-us/price-obs/price-obs-pdf.pdf)\nciteturn45search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; Object Storage ServiceBilling ... Step 3 Go back to the service list and choose Storage > Object Storage Service. ... You are billed one hour after the resource usage is calculated, so a bill may still be\nObject Storage Service\nBilling\n9 Billing Termination\nFigure 9-1 Copying the source name\nStep 3 Go back to the service list and choose Storage > Object Storage Service.\nThe bucket list is displayed.\nStep 4 In the search box above the bucket list, enter the resource name copied in Step 2\nand click the \n icon to find the resource.\nFigure 9-2 Searching for the resource\nIf no bucket is found, the bucket has been deleted.\nStep 5 Click Delete in the Operation column to delete the bucket. Ensure that the bucket\ndoes not exist in the list.\nNO TE\nYou are billed one hour after the resource usage is calculated, so a bill may still be\ngenerated after a pay-per-use resource is deleted. For instance, if you delete a pay-per-use\nresource (which is billed on an hourly basis) at 08:30, the expenditures for that hour from\n08:00 to 09:00 are usually not billed until about 10:00.\n----End\nIssue 01 (2024-08-09)\nCopyright © Huawei Cloud Computing Technologies Co., Ltd.\n70--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDelete a bucket to stop billing-Object Storage Service(OSS)-阿里云帮助中心 (https://help.aliyun.com/en/oss/user-guide/delete-buckets)\nciteturn45search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: last month; # Delete a bucket to stop billing ... Because Object Storage Service (OSS) fees are charged for the resources inside a bucket, you must clear all resources before deleting it. ... ## Assess risks before deletion ... If you have enabled cross-region replication or same-region replication, the delete operation might also clear data from the destination bucket.\n\n# Delete a bucket to stop billing\n\nUpdated at:\n\nCopy as MD\n\nProduct\n\nMy Favorites\n\nIf you no longer need a bucket, you can delete it to stop all billing. Because Object Storage Service (OSS) fees are charged for the resources inside a bucket, you must clear all resources before deleting it. Deleting a bucket is the most reliable way to prevent unexpected fees from overlooked billable resources. Note that data cannot be recovered after a bucket is deleted, and another user can claim its name after it is released. To completely stop using OSS, you must delete all buckets in your account.\n\nImage: image\n\n## Assess risks before deletion\n\n  * Risk of irreversible data loss\n\nThis action is irreversible and permanently removes all objects, their previous versions, and parts. Back up your data beforehand.\n\n  * Risk of service disruption\n\nDeleting a bucket immediately disrupts any websites, applications, or CDN services that depend on it. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUser Guide (https://support.huaweicloud.com/eu/ugobs-obs/obs-usermanual-en-pdf.pdf)\nciteturn45search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; Object Storage Service ... # Causes of Bucket Deletion Failures and Solutions ... Issue 01 (2024-08-27)  Copyright © Huawei Technologies Co., Ltd.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUser Guide (https://support.huaweicloud.com/intl/en-us/ugobs-obs/obs-usermanual-en-pdf.pdf)\nciteturn45search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; User Guide                                                                 6 Data Management ... After an object is transitioned to the Archive storage class, if it stays in this storage class for less than 90 days, you still need to pay for a full 90 days. ...   - Only transitions from the Standard storage class to the Infrequent Access storage class are supported. ... - Object deletion upon expiration:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Multi-level Caching Architecture for Stateful Stream Computation (https://vgate.cs.ucy.ac.cy/debs2022/pdfs/debs2022-20.pdf)\nciteturn45search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.4 years ago; Depending on the application scenario, the complete multi-level setup can be used where both the program cache (i.e., Caffeine) and the in-memory object store (i.e., Redis) can b